      "id": "FAM:SF32",
      "what_failed": "Optimizer-recommended rank-1 openers (all cut-centered) were found infeasible because required CUT shares far exceeded observed stretch ceilings.",
      "why": "Artifact: CUT targets ignored personnel constraints, revealing that cut-heavy profiles are rarely realizable in practice; this implies studies that rely on cut-heavy player definitions may suffer from BASE_RATE and DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY risks."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-032",
      "what_failed": "Travel x fit quadrant prediction of mover outcomes lacked convincing statistical force and suffered from methodological flaws.",
      "why": "Inconclusive outcome with LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD and OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL risks; weak continuous correlations (delta vs dest_net r=0.077) despite directional quadrants."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "The travel x fit framework provides a weakly predictive but directionally useful measure of role portability (travel score) and fit (additive/redundant/missed-need/irrelevant quadrants).",
    "Cutting is extremely sparse at the team-game level (CUT>15% in <2% of games) and heavily constrained by team offensive systems, suggesting that cut-heavy player profiles may be rare and potentially non-repeatable.",
    "Structural roles (shapers, connectors) differ in their offensive influence and portability potential.",
    "The BASE_RATE of cut-heavy play-type mixes is low, which can produce artifacts in classification and inference."
